      ":question/id": "76db17ba-163a-5fe3-9bd7-63e519e3eab2",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-158195",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1\\ge 0\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $\\ge$ sign, the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ is part of the domain. Therefore, we draw a solid curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and shade the area *outside* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/ba/ba31bbc16c58860a4bc3c4d33ea1a61cb3d86d0e73d8aa6a67869b3e96a7fe32.png)"
    },
