      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/id": "2b882cc3-ef39-5506-bf91-1860550bd797",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-158192",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,{y}^{2}<{x}^{2}\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,-|x|<y<|x|\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-|x|<y<|x|\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $<$ sign, the curves are *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed lines $y=-|x|$ and $y=|x|,$ and shade the area *below* the curve $y=|x|,$ and *above* the curve $y=-|x|.$\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/c0/c06082999e1737b6e4ea5d32a3d8d77a271e3db49c38cc3725422bcb979676d0.png)"
    },
