      ":question/problem": "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{y+{x}^{2}}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\ny+{x}^{2}\\ge 0\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,y\\ge -{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge -{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $\\ge$ sign, the curve is part of the domain. Therefore, we draw a solid curve $y=-{x}^{2}$ and shade the region *above* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/0b/0b7a6dbbab890b469cf389fa27627e9c1a5f95d8892507fcd4ba5f70541936ea.png)"
