      ":question/id": "56f9c6d4-908f-5999-9300-6cd67e4208c5",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-158600",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+y}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+y\\ne 0\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,y\\ne -{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ne -{x}^{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne$ sign, the curve is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed curve $y=-{x}^{3}$ and shade *above* and *below* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/6c/6c85783dc6626a3fea9d06431ee31e202da8c4d88e2eb1c2b6608dccdc2b782e.png)"
    },
