      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-158205",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne$ sign, the point $(0,0)$ is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we exclude the point $(x,y)=(0,0)$ and shade the plane.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/70/701c54bff1c431883f21cb980ac43f4f2f6beabb11817066a4e909d5a2663a25.png)"
